WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:19.910
MEET KEVIN:
If you’ve ever watched Meet Kevin, you’re

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:23.260
well aware of his infinitely running coupon
codes.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:28.119
In basically every video, he’ll tell you
that he’s running a massive coupon code

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:30.099
for his programs on building wealth.

00:00:30.099 --> 00:00:35.300
And if you wanna take advantage of this coupon
code, you should buy as soon as possible because

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.300
the coupon code is expiring momentarily.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.820
But, the reality is that the second that the
current coupon code expires, he’ll have

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:41.620
another one in it’s place.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:46.010
It’ll probably be for his grandmother’s
birthday or for Leif Erikson day.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:50.739
At this point, his subscribers have become
well aware of this tactic and it’s pretty

00:00:50.739 --> 00:00:52.660
much become a meme.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:56.840
You can basically go to any of his videos,
and you’ll see a bunch of comments making

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.100
fun of his coupon codes.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.430
It’s basically like the boy who cried wolf.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:06.600
When you constantly have coupon codes running,
no one’s gonna take them seriously.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.870
You might as well just price your product
lower permanently and have no coupon codes

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:12.870
right?

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:17.860
Well, it turns out that there’s actually
some truth behind Kevin’s strategy.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:23.080
You see, Kevin is by no means the only one
using this strategy.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:26.530
Likely the biggest culprit is big box retailers.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:31.330
If you search for Jeans on Kohls for example,
you’ll see that virtually every offering

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:33.979
is 30 to 50% off.

00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:39.600
You can see a similar trend with Macy’s,
JCPenny, Dillards, and basically any mall

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.600
outlet that you can think of.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.890
It’s not just these normal people stores
that employ this strategy either.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:49.729
Higher end stores like Hugo Boss are even
more guilty of this.

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:52.060
Take this regular fit suit for example.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:02:00.170
Apparently, the original price is $895, but
you can it for 50% off for no reason in the

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:02.030
middle of February.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:04.650
Must be a Valentines Day sale.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:10.330
As you can see, the retail industry is full
of this crap whether you look low or high.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:14.670
And, while it’s easy to write off Kevin
as a flip flopping salesman, writing off these

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.069
retailers is not as easy.

00:02:17.069 --> 00:02:22.860
Hugo Boss, for example, spent 80 million euros
on marketing within the first quarter of 2022

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:23.860
alone.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:26.710
So, they know exactly what they’re doing.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:30.970
They’ve made the conscious decision that
running these infinite sales are worth the

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:32.620
reputational hit that comes along with them.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:37.200
In fact, one of these retailers even tried
pricing their products more honestly and they’re

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.450
revenue ended up going down.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:46.790
So, here’s why this pesky sales strategy
is so effective and why it’s in Kevin’s

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:52.440
best interest to continue running these coupon
codes despite the public backlash.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:58.190
THE ORIGIN:
Taking a look back, the origin of modern pricing

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:01.690
startegies can be tracked back to the mid
1800s.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:06.140
The industrial revolution was in full swing
and cookie cutter department stores were replacing

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.860
mom and pop shops across the western world.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:13.879
Unlike the mom and pop shops, these department
stores carried hundred if not thousands of

00:03:13.879 --> 00:03:15.360
different products.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.830
Trying to do the good old barter with each
every customer was not only inefficient but

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:21.930
also unreliable.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:26.670
After all, one bad cashier could destroy your
profit margins.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:32.090
So, retailers decided to start implementing
fixed prices across all of their locations.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:37.230
In our modern, non confrontational world,
this would be seen as a blessing.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:41.650
You no longer had to haggle with the cashier
everytime you went grocery shopping.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:46.739
But, back in the day, fixed pricing was seen
in a much more negative light as people felt

00:03:46.739 --> 00:03:49.420
that it took away their bargaining power.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:55.400
Nonetheless, as these department stores became
ubiquitous, fixed pricing became the standard.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:58.269
At first, this was great for retailers.

00:03:58.269 --> 00:04:02.220
They didn’t have to worry about training
staff in marketing and they could pull out

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:05.939
the fairness card against any customer who
wanted a deal.

00:04:05.939 --> 00:04:11.150
They could simply say: Sir, if we gave you
a deal, it would be unfair to all of our other

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:14.549
customers, so in good consciences, we can’t
do that.

00:04:14.549 --> 00:04:20.259
In reality, a good conscience had nothing
to do with it, but this was a great scapegoat

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:22.110
for dealing with tough customers.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.090
The golden days didn’t last forever though.

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:29.360
While these retailers were able to simply
steam rollover family businesses, they couldn’t

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.530
do the same against other retail giants.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:36.630
They all had massive budgets, marketing campaigns,
and identical pricing.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:40.370
So, it became extremely difficult to stick
out.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:42.180
Enter coupons.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:46.720
If JC Penny couldn’t stick out with their
shopping experience, maybe they could stick

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.410
out with their prices.

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:51.460
There was two fatal flaws with this idea though.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:55.850
First of all, retailers didn’t actually
have very big margins to begin with.

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:59.020
Macy’s, for example, has a net margin of
3-5%.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:05.200
So, there was no way that they could run the
massive 30 to 50% discounts to get people

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:06.650
in the door.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:11.220
And even if they could, why should they have
to sell their products for a cheaper price

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:14.080
when Sears across town was charging MSRP.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:21.530
So, retailers decided, why decrease the sale
price when you could increase the MSRP.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:25.970
Consumer protection laws meant that they couldn’t
just outright lie or mislead customers about

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:27.130
the MSRP.

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:32.100
So, retailers had to come up with some pretty
clever strategies to cover their butts.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:38.030
For example, they might offer a pair of jeans
for a $100 in some remote location in Montana.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:44.290
And now they could advertise in New York that
$50 for those same jeans is 50% off.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:50.000
This strategy tapped into our inner FOMO and
our deep desire to feel special and exclusive.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.440
And before you knew it, customers were lining
up outside these stores trying to take advantage

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:57.150
of these great one time discounts.

00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:03.319
JC PENNY EXPERIMENT:
These constant flash sales did wonders when

00:06:03.319 --> 00:06:09.199
it came to getting people in the door, but
people would eventually start to notice right?

00:06:09.199 --> 00:06:13.789
And when they did, their opinion of said retailer
would just go down right?

00:06:13.789 --> 00:06:16.090
Well, yes and no.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:21.919
People did begin to notice very quickly actually,
but their reaction wasn’t what you might

00:06:21.919 --> 00:06:23.039
expect.

00:06:23.039 --> 00:06:28.590
On one side, yes people did become numb to
sales and they started feeling: why don’t

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:32.389
these retailers just put up more honest pricing
to begin with.

00:06:32.389 --> 00:06:37.220
But, this numbness led to another, much stronger
feeling as well.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:41.669
Given that everyone is doing deals all the
time, it’s not worth buying products that

00:06:41.669 --> 00:06:45.970
are notorious for this like clothing and kitchen
items for MSRP.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:51.310
It’s probably just a week or two until that
product goes on sale itself, and they could

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:52.580
just buy it then.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:59.300
In other words, sales had indeed lost all
meaning, but a lack of a sale very much had

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:00.300
meaning.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.800
A lack of a sale meant that the given price
was a ripoff.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:08.890
And given that this strategy has been engrained
in our culture for several decades now, all

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:10.870
of us feel this way to a certain a degree.

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:15.720
I mean, it’s no coincidence that black friday
sales have grown to encompass the entirety

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:17.389
of the 4th quarter.

00:07:17.389 --> 00:07:22.039
And it’s not random that Amazon suddenly
has two Prime days per year.

00:07:22.039 --> 00:07:28.360
The reality is that, though seemingly useless,
these arbitrary discounts drive sales, and

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:31.380
a lack of them drives losses.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:35.599
And this isn’t just some sort of marketing
theory either.

00:07:35.599 --> 00:07:40.569
Ron Johnson’s disastrous reign at JCPenny
has given us real world data.

00:07:40.569 --> 00:07:45.860
You’re probably not familiar with Ron himself,
but I’m sure you’re familiar with his

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:46.860
work.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.180
Ron is the architect of the Apple store.

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:54.259
He joined Apple in January of 2000 as Senior
Vice President of retail operations.

00:07:54.259 --> 00:07:59.760
And by the time he left, he had propelled
Apple stores to $4,032 per square foot annually.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:05.660
So, clearly, this guy knew what he was doing,
and that’s why JCPenny would hire him as

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:07.610
CEO in late 2011.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:13.740
Ron was given that task of transforming JCPenny,
and almost immediately, he was drawn towards

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.849
their constant markdowns.

00:08:15.849 --> 00:08:20.009
Like most of us, he felt that this was the
21st century.

00:08:20.009 --> 00:08:21.979
The internet is a thing.

00:08:21.979 --> 00:08:23.879
People can price check on the go.

00:08:23.879 --> 00:08:27.539
So, why keep up these braindead list prices.

00:08:27.539 --> 00:08:33.300
Well, the answer is seemingly no reason at
all, so Ron went ahead and eliminated all

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:35.899
markdowns across the entire company.

00:08:35.899 --> 00:08:40.640
Instead, he introduced “fair and square”
pricing which is exactly what it sounds like.

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:44.050
He also eliminated the use of 99 in price
tags.

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:48.209
Surely, none of us are stupid enough to base
our purchasing decision on these arbitrary

00:08:48.209 --> 00:08:50.960
deals or 1 cent right.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:55.170
If anything, consumers should appreciate the
transparent pricing.

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:58.120
Oh, how wrong he was.

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:04.279
Within just 12 months, same store sales would
decline 25% and the company would end up losing

00:09:04.279 --> 00:09:05.940
$1 billion.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:11.769
Ron’s performance was so bad that it was
literally the worst performance of any retail

00:09:11.769 --> 00:09:13.200
CEO ever.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.550
And, this blunder would put JCPenny on the
track towards bankruptcy.

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:23.970
I don’t think you’d be surprised to hear
that Ron would be fired just 4 months later.

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:29.839
So, what went wrong?

00:09:29.839 --> 00:09:32.290
THE TAKEAWAY:
The main factor that Ron had failed to consider

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:37.570
was the radically different objectives of
Apple stores and JCPenny outlets.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:40.100
Here’s the thing, Apple stores don’t need
to sell you on their products.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:44.149
iPhones sell themselves, and people who go
to Apple stores have usually already made

00:09:44.149 --> 00:09:45.149
up their mind.

00:09:45.149 --> 00:09:48.019
They’re there to buy or to browse.

00:09:48.019 --> 00:09:51.279
There’s not very many people who are on
the fence.

00:09:51.279 --> 00:09:57.110
So, the entire point of Apple stores is to
simply create an enjoyable shopping experience.

00:09:57.110 --> 00:10:02.970
Meanwhile, JCPenny is trying to sell extraordinarily
oversaturated products in massive upscale

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:04.160
locations.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:08.020
Shoppers at JCPenny aren’t usually there
with intention.

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:12.060
Many of them are just strolling around the
mall and decided to stop by.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:17.079
So, JCPenny has to work very hard to get these
strollers to become customers.

00:10:17.079 --> 00:10:22.520
The same case could be said about iPhones
as well outside of Apple stores.

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:28.850
If you go into AT&amp;T, Verizon, or TMobile,
you’ll see dozens of deals, trade in discounts,

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:31.519
and of course aggressive salesmen.

00:10:31.519 --> 00:10:36.700
Selling YouTube courses is much closer to
JCPenny than it is to Apple.

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:39.080
People are watching YouTube videos in their
leisure time.

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:42.380
They just want to be entertained and maybe
informed.

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:48.300
But, they’re definitely not jumping up and
down about the prospect of buying a $1,700

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:49.300
course.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:54.519
We should also mention that basically every
finance YouTuber has their own money course

00:10:54.519 --> 00:10:57.650
and their own YouTube course.

00:10:57.650 --> 00:11:03.389
So, Kevin not only has to coninvce a casual
viewer to buy an expensive product, but he

00:11:03.389 --> 00:11:07.080
also has to convince them to buy his product
over someone elses.

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:09.000
Enter coupon codes.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:14.230
Offering a quote on quote $1700 course for
$500 accomplishes two things.

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:17.260
First of all, it creates a sense of urgency.

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:21.370
People who watch Kevin regularly know that
another coupon is just around the corner,

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:25.470
but new viewers will naturally feel that they
have a limited time to take advantage of this

00:11:25.470 --> 00:11:26.800
opportunity.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:30.950
We should also mention that out of his regular
viewers, the people who are willing to buy

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:34.279
his course have probably already bought it.

00:11:34.279 --> 00:11:40.370
So, a coupon code or a lack of a coupon code
wouldn’t really sway his regular viewers,

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:44.140
but it does wonders with new viewers.

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:49.060
Aside from creating urgency, the listing price
also creates a high price anchor.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:53.170
If you were judging the course based on just
it’s content, you might feel that it’s

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:54.390
only worth $50.

00:11:54.390 --> 00:12:00.660
But, since you’re given this initial price
of $1700, $500 seems a lot more reasonable.

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:05.220
Even if you were looking for a more aggressive
deal, you’d likely be aiming for $400 or

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:07.920
$300, not $50.

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:14.370
And let’s just say, this strategy has worked
extraordinarily well for Kevin.

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:19.570
THE AFTERMATH:
You can argue all you want about marketing

00:12:19.570 --> 00:12:26.430
strategies and brand perception, but something
you can’t argue with is results, and Kevin’s

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:29.300
got the best results in the space period.

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:35.070
Graham Stephan for example is the largest
finance YouTuber with over 4 million subscribers.

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:41.709
And in 2021, he made $6.1 million which is
an extraordinary amount of money.

00:12:41.709 --> 00:12:46.580
Meet Kevin, however, has less than half of
Graham’s subscriber count, yet he was making

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:49.940
$3 million per month in 2021.

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:52.649
Not to mention he now owns a private jet.

00:12:52.649 --> 00:12:58.389
Now, let me be clear, this is not an endorsement
of Meet Kevin or his courses.

00:12:58.389 --> 00:13:02.960
Rather, it’s simply a discussion about how
effective his coupon code strategy is despite

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:05.910
the fact that he’s always offering them.

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:12.220
The bottom line is that while annoying, it’s
effective, and the only thing we can blame

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:13.769
is our own psychology.

00:13:13.769 --> 00:13:18.949
At the end of the day, Kevin is just using
the psychology of money to sell a course about

00:13:18.949 --> 00:13:21.329
the psychology of money.

00:13:21.329 --> 00:13:24.080
Along those lines, I have a question for you.

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:26.970
Would you buy jeans that aren’t on sale?

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:28.220
Comment that down below.

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:32.040
Also, drop a like if you would like to help
my psychology.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:36.300
And of course, consider checking out our discord
community to suggest future video ideas and

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:42.430
consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

